            <title>[LU-20859] 'lfs find --printf' mishandles flags in field width directives</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20859</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;For various format strings which can be handed to a command like "&lt;tt&gt;lfs find $path --printf $format_string"&lt;/tt&gt;, the handling of directive width parsing is incorrect or inconsistent, either between validate_printf_str() and parse_format_width(), or with GNU find.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Example erroneous cases:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="code panel" style="border-width: 1px;"&gt;&lt;div class="codeContent panelContent"&gt;
&lt;pre class="code-java"&gt;
&lt;span class="code-quote"&gt;"% -6p"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="code-comment"&gt;// &lt;span class="code-quote"&gt;'-'&lt;/span&gt; not detected, &lt;span class="code-quote"&gt;'-6'&lt;/span&gt; parsed as negative number, incidentally is treated the same as &lt;span class="code-keyword"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; the width was parsed as &lt;span class="code-quote"&gt;'1023'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="code-quote"&gt;"%+6p"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="code-comment"&gt;// validate_printf_str() prints warning however parse_format_width() accepts it normally
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="code-quote"&gt;"% 6p"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="code-comment"&gt;// validate_printf_str() prints warning however parse_format_width() accepts it normally&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Beyond those two cases which definitely should be fixed, there are other inconsistencies with how GNU find handles width parameters:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The # char is not accepted&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The + char is only accepted when immediately followed by a digit&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Space with no width digit after ("% p") is rejected&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Precision, i.e. "%.5p" not implemented (I think leaving this out is fine, doesn't seem very useful)&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;parse_format_width() accepts a greater range of whitespace characters (space, \t, \n, \r, \v, \f) than GNU find (space)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;


&lt;p&gt;I think the simplest solution here is to reject whitespace / +. It doesn't seem necessary to have full parity with GNU find, as much as a strict implementation of the needed subset. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;</description>
